    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A few years ago, most Kenyans met partners through school, church, work or family introductions. Today, many people also meet through their phones. If you are curious about online dating in Kenya, or you tried it once and found it confusing, this guide walks you through the whole process in plain language. We cover how it works, how to pick a platform, how to build a profile, what to say, how to stay safe, how to handle the first meeting and the habits that lead to better results.</p>
<p>You do not need to be tech-savvy. You need patience, honesty and a bit of caution. Let us start with the basics.</p>

<h2>How online dating in Kenya works</h2>
<p>The idea is simple. You create a profile with photos and a short bio, then you browse other people's profiles, send messages and see who replies. Some platforms suggest people near you. Others let you search by age, location or interests. When you both feel there is a connection, you chat, perhaps move to a call, and eventually meet in person.</p>
<p>Online dating is not only about romance. People use these platforms to find a serious partner, to date casually without pressure, to meet friends in a new town or to connect with people abroad. Be clear with yourself about what you want, because that helps you choose the right people and write an honest profile.</p>
<p>The big advantage is reach. In a busy city like Nairobi, or a smaller town like Nyeri, you may never cross paths with someone who shares your interests. A good platform puts those people in front of you. The challenge is that you cannot see who is real or trustworthy at first glance, which is why the rest of this guide stresses care and common sense.</p>

<h2>Choosing a platform wisely</h2>
<p>Not all platforms are equal. Before you create an account, check a few things:</p>
<ul>
<li>Verification: Does the platform check that profiles belong to real people? Read our article on <a href="/blog/why-profile-verification-matters">why profile verification matters</a>.</li>
<li>Safety tools: Can you block and report someone easily?</li>
<li>Clear pricing: Is it obvious what is free and what you pay for?</li>
<li>Local focus: Are the people near you and genuinely in Kenya, or scattered across the world?</li>
<li>Privacy: Does it explain how your photos and details are used?</li>
</ul>
<p>Spend ten minutes reading the <a href="/faq">frequently asked questions</a> and the <a href="/about">about page</a> of any platform you consider. Be wary of any service that promises instant love, pressures you to pay at once or asks you to move to another app on the first day. If something feels too good to be true, it usually is.</p>

<h2>Building a profile that gets attention</h2>
<p>Your profile is your first impression, and it has two main parts: photos and words.</p>
<p>For photos, use clear, recent pictures in good light. One should show your face clearly and one should show you doing something you enjoy. Avoid group photos where nobody can tell which person you are, heavy filters, and pictures of other people. Our guide to <a href="/blog/best-dating-profile-photos">the best dating profile photos</a> goes into detail.</p>
<p>For your bio, write like you speak. Mention two or three real interests, such as hiking, gospel music or football, your work or studies in general terms, and what you are looking for. A short, warm bio beats a long list of demands. Take a look at <a href="/blog/how-to-write-a-dating-profile-bio">how to write a dating profile bio</a> and the common <a href="/blog/dating-profile-mistakes-to-avoid">dating profile mistakes to avoid</a>.</p>
<p>Be honest about your age, location and intentions. Small exaggerations cause awkward moments later, and people notice. Honesty also attracts the kind of person you really want to meet.</p>

<h2>Starting conversations and keeping them going</h2>
<p>The first message matters. "Hi" or "hey" rarely gets a reply, because it gives the other person nothing to respond to. Instead, mention something specific from their profile and ask a simple question. For example: "I saw you love hiking Ngong Hills. Do you have a favourite spot?" Our guides on <a href="/blog/five-first-message-openers">five first message openers</a> and <a href="/blog/how-to-start-a-conversation-online">how to start a conversation online</a> give plenty of examples.</p>
<p>Once the chat is going, keep it balanced. Ask questions, answer yours properly and share something about yourself. Do not send many messages in a row if they have not replied, and do not read too much into slow replies. People work, study and live busy lives. For pacing, see <a href="/blog/texting-rules-early-dating">texting rules for early dating</a>.</p>
<p>If the conversation is going well after some days, you can suggest a short call or an outing. A kind way to do it is shown in <a href="/blog/how-to-ask-someone-out-over-text">how to ask someone out over text</a>. If they say no, thank them and move on. Learning to <a href="/blog/how-to-handle-rejection-gracefully">handle rejection gracefully</a> will save you stress and keep your self-esteem intact.</p>

<h2>Staying safe from the start</h2>
<p>Most people online are genuine, but a small number are not, and the safest approach is to assume nothing until you have reason to trust. Keep these rules in mind from the first message:</p>
<ul>
<li>Never send money, airtime or mobile payments to someone you have not met, whatever the reason.</li>
<li>Do not share your home address, workplace details, ID or financial information early.</li>
<li>Be suspicious of anyone who says "I love you" within days, or who always has an emergency.</li>
<li>Insist on a live video call before meeting. Someone who refuses every time may not be who they say they are.</li>
<li>Use the block and report tools whenever someone makes you uncomfortable.</li>
