<p>Read our guides to <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-fake-profiles-and-catfishing">spotting fake profiles and catfishing</a>, <a href="/blog/romance-scams-in-kenya-warning-signs">romance scam warning signs</a> and <a href="/blog/protect-your-privacy-on-dating-apps">protecting your privacy on dating apps</a>. The platform's own <a href="/safety-tips">safety tips</a> are worth a few minutes before you start.</p>

<h2>Money, plans and M-Pesa in general terms</h2>
<p>Many dating platforms are free to join and browse, with paid plans that unlock extra features such as more messaging or visibility. Before you pay, check exactly what each plan includes, how long it lasts and how to cancel. You can see how ConnectHub explains its options on the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>. Remember that paying for a plan is a payment to the platform, not to another person.</p>
<p>In Kenya, mobile money is how most people pay for things, and that is also true on dating platforms. Use the official payment option inside the platform, check the payee name before you confirm, and keep your confirmation message. Never send M-Pesa directly to someone you met online, even for something small. Requests like "send me airtime", "help with hospital bills" or "pay the clearing fee for my gift" are classic tricks. Read <a href="/blog/should-you-send-money-to-someone-you-met-online">should you send money to someone you met online</a> to understand why.</p>

<h2>Verifying someone and planning the first meeting</h2>
<p>Before you meet, do a quick reality check. Look for consistent photos, a profile that matches what they say, and a willingness to talk on the phone or video. A short video call is the single best test. If they will not show their face live, or keep making excuses, pause the conversation.</p>
<p>Our article on <a href="/blog/how-to-verify-someone-is-real-before-meeting">how to verify someone is real before meeting</a> lists simple steps. Trust your gut as well. If something feels off, it is fine to stop, even if you cannot explain why.</p>

<p>Once you are happy they are real, plan the meeting carefully. Choose a public, busy place in daylight or early evening, such as a cafe, a mall, a garden or a restaurant, and arrange your own transport so you can leave when you want. Tell a friend where you are going and who you are meeting, and share your live location if you can. Do not accept a lift from a first date, and keep your drink in sight.</p>
<p>Keep the first meeting short, perhaps an hour or two, so there is no pressure. Ideas are in our guides to <a href="/blog/first-date-ideas-in-nairobi">first date ideas in Nairobi</a>, <a href="/blog/first-date-ideas-outside-nairobi">first date ideas outside Nairobi</a> and <a href="/blog/first-date-conversation-topics">first date conversation topics</a>. Use the <a href="/blog/meet-someone-safely">safety checklist for meeting someone</a> beforehand.</p>

<h2>Culture, family and expectations</h2>
<p>Dating in Kenya often involves family, community and faith in ways that differ from other places. Some people are open about dating, while others keep it private until things are serious. Be respectful of these differences, and be honest early about what matters to you, whether that is religion, children, location or marriage.</p>
<p>You will meet people who speak English, Swahili, Sheng or their mother tongue, often mixed in the same chat. Meet people where they are, and avoid assumptions about someone because of their background. Our guide to <a href="/blog/dating-etiquette-in-kenya">dating etiquette in Kenya</a> covers manners, who pays and public versus private behaviour, and <a href="/blog/introducing-your-partner-to-family-in-kenya">introducing your partner to family in Kenya</a> helps when things get serious.</p>

<p>Timing matters too. Punctuality is a sign of respect, so arrive on time or send a message if you are delayed, especially if you are coming by matatu through Nairobi traffic. On the first date, it is common and kind for the person who invited to offer to pay, but many people prefer to split the bill, and either is fine if you talk about it openly and without pressure.</p>

<h2>Common pitfalls and how to avoid them</h2>
<p>Most frustrations in online dating are avoidable. Watch for these patterns:</p>
<ol>
<li>Chasing too many chats at once, so nobody gets real attention.</li>
<li>Judging people only on photos instead of how they communicate.</li>
<li>Moving too fast, or letting someone else rush you.</li>
<li>Ignoring red flags because you are lonely or hopeful. See <a href="/blog/green-flags-and-red-flags-in-early-dating">green flags and red flags in early dating</a>.</li>
<li>Giving up after a few disappointments. Dating takes patience.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you have recently ended a relationship, give yourself time first. The guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-know-if-you-are-ready-to-date-again">how to know if you are ready to date again</a> can help you decide. And when you have found someone you like, the <a href="/blog/define-the-relationship-talk">define the relationship talk</a> is an honest way to move forward.</p>

<h2>Meeting people abroad, and taking the first step</h2>
